          <p>
            Two caveats on Premier's panel. The Target nutrition panel says 3 g of carbohydrate, while some grocery listings show 4 g, which may mean the label was revised; and Target's product description says 160 calories while its panel says 150. We used the panel figures. On ISO100, the 5 lb tub was listed at Vitacost but shown as temporarily unavailable on the day we checked, which matters for the cost comparison below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Did You Mean Premier Protein Shakes? How the Carton Compares to ISO100
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you searched "Dymatize vs Premier Protein" thinking of the bottled shake, the short answer is that Premier's 30 g shake and Dymatize ISO100 do different jobs: one is a carton you open, the other a powder you mix. Per <a href="https://www.premierprotein.com/classic-protein-shakes" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Premier's Classic shake page</a> at the time of writing, each Classic shake gives 30 g of protein and 160 calories with 1 g of sugar, no added sugar and 24 vitamins and minerals.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes the carton a convenience product. You pay for the packaging, the shelf stability and the fact that you can drink it in a car or at a desk without a shaker. When we priced a 12-pack of chocolate cartons on Amazon for our <a href="/premier-protein-vs-optimum-nutrition/">Premier Protein vs Optimum Nutrition</a> comparison on November 20, it came to about $1.86 per 25 g of protein: more than Premier's own powder at the Target price in our database, but still less than ISO100 at the Vitacost price.
          </p>

          <p>
            The shake also carries 30 g of protein against ISO100's 25 g per scoop, so for a single grab-and-go serving it delivers more. What it does not give you is control. ISO100 lets you set the dose, mix it into oats or coffee, and choose between water and milk. It is also isolate-only, which keeps lactose low; we have not checked the carton's full ingredient list for this page, so read it on the box if lactose is your concern.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="same-company" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Are Dymatize and Premier Protein Made by the Same Company?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Both brands belong to <a href="https://www.bellring.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">BellRing Brands</a>, which lists Premier Protein, Dymatize and PowerBar as its brands on its website. That surprises a lot of shoppers who see the two as rivals, and it explains why the powders are positioned so differently instead of competing head-on.
          </p>

          <p>
            BellRing describes Premier Protein as a brand of ready-to-drink shakes and powders for the everyday health shopper, and Dymatize as a performance nutrition brand. The products follow that split. Premier's powder is a mainstream whey blend sold next to its shakes in supermarkets, with flavors named after milkshakes. ISO100 is a hydrolyzed isolate sold mainly through sports nutrition retailers and Amazon, with a certification aimed at athletes and licensed flavors aimed at gym-goers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shared ownership does not mean shared formulas or shared testing. The certification on ISO100 applies to that product, not to the parent company, and we found no sport certification claimed for Premier's powder. Judge each tub on its own label, which is what the rest of this page does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Has More Protein per Scoop, Dymatize or Premier Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein has more protein per serving, 30 g against 25 g, but Dymatize ISO100 has more protein per gram of powder. Per the labels in our database, ISO100 fits 25 g of protein into a 32 g scoop, which is 78 percent protein by weight. Premier fits 30 g into a 41 g serving, which is 73 percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference is the serving. Premier's 41 g serving is about two scoops, so its "30 g" is a bigger dose, not a more concentrated powder. If you used a 41 g portion of ISO100, you would get about 32 g of protein. The extra 5 percentage points of density in ISO100 come from using only isolate, which is filtered further than the whey concentrate in Premier's blend and carries less fat, carbohydrate and lactose with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories per gram of protein are almost identical. ISO100 gives 25 g for 120 calories, and Premier gives 30 g for 150 calories, which scales to 125 calories per 25 g. Neither powder is meaningfully better for a calorie budget; the gap is about the size of a single almond.
          </p>

          <p>
            ISO100 lists 2.6 g of leucine per scoop on the listing we used, and Premier does not disclose a leucine figure. That does not mean Premier is short on leucine, since whey isolate and concentrate are both naturally rich in it, but it is one reason ISO100 scores higher on our formula: we reward labels that show their amino acids. If hitting a protein target in fewer servings is your main goal, Premier's 30 g serving is simpler; if you want the purest powder per scoop, ISO100 wins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Is Cheaper per Gram of Protein, Dymatize or Premier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein is clearly cheaper per gram: about $1.47 per 25 g of protein against about $2.77 for Dymatize ISO100, using the retailer prices in our database checked on November 1, 2026. That is almost half the cost for the same protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The arithmetic is simple. Premier's 24.5 oz tub was $29.99 at Target for 17 servings of 30 g, which is 510 g of protein or 20.4 portions of 25 g; $29.99 divided by 20.4 is about $1.47. ISO100's 5 lb tub was listed at $196.72 at Vitacost for about 71 servings of 25 g, so each 25 g portion costs about $2.77. Put another way, a dollar buys about 17 g of protein from Premier and about 9 g from ISO100 at those prices.
          </p>

          <p>
            That ISO100 price deserves a warning. The Vitacost 5 lb tub was marked temporarily unavailable when we checked, and its list price looks high for the size: the same retailer's 3 lb tub was $126.64 and its 1.43 lb tub $59.41, which work out to about $42 per pound each against about $39 per pound for the 5 lb. ISO100 is often cheaper elsewhere. In our <a href="/ryse-vs-ghost-protein/">Ryse vs Ghost comparison</a>, a 3 lb Fruity Pebbles tub on Amazon came to about $2.14 per 25 g on November 20. Even at that price, Premier stays cheaper per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The up-front cost runs the other way, as it often does. Premier's small tub is a $30 purchase, which makes it easy to try. ISO100's 5 lb tub is a large commitment, though it lasts more than four times as long. If you are comparing shelf prices in a store, divide by grams of protein before you decide. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> roundup lists cheaper isolates if you want ISO100's lactose profile for less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Dymatize or Premier Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dymatize ISO100 is the one with a sport certification: retailer listings, including the Vitacost page we used, show the Informed Choice logo. We could not confirm the exact flavors and lots in the Informed Choice registry, which returned an error when we tried, so check the <a href="https://choice.wetestyoutrust.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Informed Choice site</a> for your flavor before you rely on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier Protein powder makes no sport certification claim that we could find. Target lists it as gluten-free and kosher, but no certifying body was named, so we record those as claims rather than certifications. We also did not find Premier's powder in the <a href="https://www.nsfsport.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">NSF Certified for Sport</a> listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why it matters: Informed Choice and similar programs test samples for substances banned in sport. For athletes in tested competition, a certified product is the main defense against an accidental positive from a contaminated tub. For everyone else, certification is a quality signal rather than a requirement, and an uncertified mainstream whey from a large manufacturer is not evidence of a problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Has the Cleaner Label and Is Easier on the Stomach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dymatize ISO100 is the gentler choice for most sensitive stomachs, while Premier Protein has the friendlier allergen list for people avoiding soy. Which one is "cleaner" depends on what you are trying to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            On lactose, ISO100 wins. Its protein is hydrolyzed whey isolate and whey isolate only, and isolate is filtered to remove most of the milk sugar. Premier's blend includes whey concentrate, which carries more lactose; the label does not say how much. If concentrate-based shakes leave you bloated, ISO100 is the safer bet, and our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> explainer covers the difference in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            On additives, the two are close. ISO100 lists cocoa, salt, soy lecithin, natural and artificial flavor, potassium chloride, sucralose and stevia. Premier lists cocoa, soluble corn fiber, sunflower lecithin, natural and artificial flavors, acesulfame potassium, sucralose, xanthan gum and salt. Premier's xanthan gum is the one ingredient our Label Score flags, mainly because some people find gums hard on digestion. ISO100's soy lecithin is a soy allergen, which Premier avoids by using sunflower lecithin.
          </p>

          <p>
            On sweeteners, both use sucralose. ISO100 pairs it with stevia, and Premier with acesulfame potassium. If you avoid all artificial sweeteners, neither is for you; if you only want to avoid acesulfame potassium, ISO100 is the pick. Both list 1 g of sugar per serving, so neither is a sugar problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier, Dymatize or Premier Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyer reviews generally describe ISO100 as mixing faster and thinner, and Premier as thicker and more milkshake-like, so the better taste depends on the texture you prefer. We have not tasted either for this page; this section summarises what buyers and retailer listings report.
          </p>

          <p>
            ISO100's thin texture is typical of isolate. With little fat and almost no lactose, it dissolves quickly in water and does not get gluey, which buyers often praise for post-workout shakes and for stirring into coffee or yogurt. The flip side is the most common complaint: in water it can taste watery to people used to concentrate blends. Mixing it with milk changes that, at the cost of extra calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier's xanthan gum and soluble corn fiber are there partly for body, which is why buyers tend to describe it as creamier. Its flavor names, Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Milkshake, Café Latte, Strawberry Milkshake and Chocolate Peanut Butter, mirror the shakes, and people who like the cartons often say the powder tastes close. Two scoops also means more powder to dissolve, so a blender or a good shaker helps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavor range favors Dymatize. Retailer listings show about a dozen ISO100 flavors, including Gourmet Chocolate, Gourmet Vanilla, Fudge Brownie, Cookies and Cream, licensed Fruity Pebbles and Cocoa Pebbles, and Dunkin' Cappuccino and Glazed Donut. Premier's classic powder line has five. If variety keeps you consistent, ISO100 gives you more to rotate. For more on Premier's range, see our <a href="/best-premier-protein-flavor/">best Premier Protein flavor</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dymatize-strengths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Dymatize ISO100 Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            ISO100 does three things well: it packs 25 g of protein into a 32 g scoop, it keeps lactose low by using isolate only, and it carries a recognised sport certification on retailer listings. That combination is why it shows up on so many athlete and cutting lists, and why it scores 64 out of 100 on our Label Score, four points above Premier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it falls short is cost and texture. At the Vitacost price in our database it is nearly twice the cost per gram of Premier, and even at the lower Amazon price we saw on a smaller tub it is still more expensive. Buyers who want a creamy shake in water often find it thin, and anyone avoiding soy has to read past the soy lecithin. Our full <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize protein powder review</a> covers the rest of the range, including Elite.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="premier-strengths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. What Premier Protein Powder Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier's powder does two things very well: it is cheap per gram, at about $1.47 per 25 g in our database, and it gives a full 30 g of protein per serving. It is also soy-free, easy to find in ordinary supermarkets, and familiar to anyone who already likes the shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            The blend leads with whey protein isolate before concentrate on the ingredient list, so it is leaner than many budget wheys, and at 150 calories for 30 g of protein it suits weight-loss plans as well as ISO100 does. For a household buying protein on a budget, it is hard to beat at a supermarket.
          </p>

          <p>
            It falls short on testing and on scale. We found no sport certification, so it is the wrong pick for tested athletes. The whey concentrate makes it less suitable for lactose-sensitive users, the 17-serving tub runs out in two and a half weeks at one serving a day, and the flavor list is short. Our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein powder review</a> goes through each flavor and size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Which Should You Buy, Dymatize or Premier Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Premier Protein powder unless you have a specific reason to pay for ISO100. Those reasons are real for some buyers, so here is how we would split it by situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drug-tested athletes:</strong> ISO100, and confirm your flavor on the Informed Choice site. Premier has no certification we could find. <strong>Lactose-sensitive:</strong> ISO100, since it is isolate only. <strong>Tight budget:</strong> Premier, at roughly half the cost per gram in our data. <strong>Soy allergy:</strong> Premier, which uses sunflower lecithin; ISO100 lists soy lecithin.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hitting a high daily protein target:</strong> Premier's 30 g serving gets there in fewer shakes, though ISO100 is denser per gram of powder. <strong>Cutting:</strong> it is close to a tie on calories per gram of protein; pick ISO100 if bloating is part of your problem. <strong>Variety:</strong> ISO100, with about a dozen flavors. <strong>No time to mix:</strong> neither powder; buy Premier's 30 g shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            15. Who Should Skip Both Dymatize and Premier Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are vegan or dairy-allergic, since both are whey from milk; a plant protein is the right category for you. Skip both if you avoid all artificial sweeteners, because both use sucralose. And skip both if you want an unflavored, single-ingredient whey for cooking, since the flagship flavors here are sweetened.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want ISO100's isolate profile at a lower price, or a certified powder that is soy-free, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> roundup ranks the alternatives by cost per 25 g and testing. And if your real comparison is Premier against a bigger gym brand, the <a href="/premier-protein-vs-optimum-nutrition/">Premier vs Optimum Nutrition</a> breakdown is the better page.
          </p>
        </div>

