        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing the 30 g serving with the 25 g scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premier's 30 g comes from a 41 g, two-scoop serving, while ISO100's 25 g comes from one 32 g scoop. Gram for gram of powder, ISO100 is the denser product. Compare protein per gram of powder or cost per 25 g of protein, not the headline number on the front of the tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging cost by the price on the shelf</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $30 tub of Premier looks cheap next to a $197 tub of ISO100, and per gram it is, but the reverse can happen with other sizes and sales. Divide the price by total grams of protein before buying. In our data, Premier was about $1.47 per 25 g and ISO100 about $2.77.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming shared ownership means shared testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dymatize and Premier Protein both belong to BellRing Brands, but the Informed Choice logo on ISO100 listings applies to that product only. We found no sport certification for Premier's powder. Tested athletes should check the registry for the exact product and flavor, not the parent company.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Premier for a lactose problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premier's protein blend includes whey concentrate, which carries more lactose than isolate, and the label does not say how much. If dairy shakes bloat you, an isolate-only powder like ISO100 is the safer test. If even isolate bothers you, a lactose-free or plant protein is the next step.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing the shake with the powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premier's ready-to-drink shake and its powder have different labels and prices. The shake gives 30 g of protein in 160 calories per Premier, the powder 30 g in 150 calories per Target's panel. Reviews, prices and nutrition you read online may describe the carton, so check which product the source means.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
